## week 3

### 1. simple calcx — undo needs receipts 😹
The bigger thing was undo. Ermm, just remembering the last operator wouldn't be enough: multiplying by zero wipes out information, so dividing afterwards can't bring it back. Instead, before each successful calculation, I save the old total in `historyx`. Undo pops that value. Multiple undos work cos the list holds multiple old totals. Invalid numbers, unknown operators and division by zero don't enter the history at all. `float()` handles decimal inputs; NaN/infinity are rejected so the total stays usable.

### 2. palindromex — let the pointers do the walking

Reversing the sentence would've been the obvious shortcut, but they banned reversed strings AND cleaned substrings 😭. So I start one pointer at the beginning and one at the end. If the left character is punctuation, move left inward. If the right one is punctuation, move right inward. Only compare when both are letters/digits, and lowercase those individual characters for the comparison.

For “Was it a car or a cat I saw?”, the punctuation gets skipped where it lives. Nothing needs copying into a cleaned sentence. A mismatch ends things immediately; matching characters move both pointers inward. If they meet/cross, every relevant pair matched. Empty text works too cos there are no mismatching pairs to find.

### 3. gradez — no sum(), max() or min()? okay then 😹

They took away sum() for the average and max()/min() for the ranking. Ermm, an average is still just a running total divided by the number of scores, so I add each score myself. The grade checks go from A downward. That matters with decimal averages: 69.9 is below 70 but above 60, so it's B without rounding it into another grade.

As each student's result arrives, I compare it with the current highest/lowest rows. The first scored student fills both slots; later students replace a slot only if they're strictly better/worse. That also leaves ties with the first student. Starting with zero would've been awkward for the lowest tracker, so `None` means “haven't seen a scored student yet.” No scores gets N/A rather than pretending they earned zero.

### 4. second_largest — two slots, one lap

Sort the numbers and take the second one? Nice thought, banned route 😹. Also, the second item after sorting might just be another copy of the largest. The constraint wants the second-largest UNIQUE number in one pass, so I keep a largest slot and a runner-up slot.

When a new largest turns up, the old largest becomes runner-up. Otherwise, the number can improve runner-up only if it differs from the largest. For `[4, 9, 2, 9, 7]`, the second 9 changes nothing and 7 takes the runner-up spot. I use `None` for empty slots; using zero would break negative-only lists. If runner-up stays `None`, there weren't two distinct numbers.

### 5. word_frex — punctuation can't glue words together

No regex, no Counter, no string.punctuation, and no max() for the winner. Aight 😼. I wrote the punctuation characters explicitly and replace each one with a space while lowercasing the text. Deleting every punctuation mark outright would turn `python,python` into `pythonpython`, so making a gap is the useful choice here. That policy also splits contractions/hyphenated words at punctuation.

Then each word gets a dictionary entry: existing count plus one, or zero plus one if it's new. To find the winner, I walk through that dictionary with a highest-count variable. Strictly greater replaces the winner; equal counts leave it alone. In the supplied example, python and is both appear twice, but python arrived first, so python wins as expected.

### 6. two_sumx — ask for what's missing

Checking every number against every other number would find a pair, but the nested loops were banned and the target was O(n). Instead of asking “what pairs with everything?”, I ask “what exact value would complete this number?” For target 9 and current number 7, that's 2.

`seenx` maps earlier numbers to their indices. If the missing value is there, return its index and the current index. Otherwise save the current number. Checking BEFORE saving is the lil trick that stops one position pairing with itself; `[3]` can't make 6, but `[3, 3]` can. Keeping the earliest index for duplicate numbers makes the first-pair choice consistent. Dictionary lookups are average O(1), so the scan is average O(n).

### 7. anagramz — letters need their attendance count

Sorting both words would make comparison easy, but sorted() and Counter weren't allowed. Just checking whether both words contain the same letters wouldn't be enough either: `aab` and `abb` share their letter types but have different counts.

So each word gets its own lowercase character-frequency dictionary. `listen` and `silent` produce identical maps, while `apple` and `pale` don't cos apple has two p's. Comparing the dictionaries checks both the characters and their counts. I don't strip spaces or punctuation here; this task says words and only asks us to ignore casing.

### 8. cipherx — the alphabet is a circle now 😼

The challenge was wrap-around while leaving spaces, digits and punctuation alone. A plain character-code addition would move z past the alphabet, so I first turn the letter into a position from 0 to 25. Add the shift, take `% 26`, then move that position back into its character-code range.

Uppercase uses A as its starting point and lowercase uses a. Everything outside those English-letter ranges passes through unchanged. For decryption, I negate the shift and use the very same calculation. That means backwards wrap-around and huge shifts don't need separate rules; modulo handles both. The script asks for the mode before applying it.

### 9. number_reverze — strings weren't invited 😭

They said no converting the integer to a string. So the digits have to come out through maths: `% 10` gives the last digit, and `// 10` removes it. Each extracted digit goes onto the result with `result * 10 + digit`.

With 123, that builds 3, then 32, then 321. I handle the sign first and work on a positive magnitude, cos Python's floor division on negatives would make the peeling logic awkward. Afterwards I restore the sign. For 1200 the starting zeros contribute nothing, so the result is 21 naturally. Zero never enters the loop and stays zero.

## week 4

### 1. palindromex again — same walk, cleaner jobs

The two-pointer restrictions still apply, but now the functions have to return values and keep print() outside. `is_letter` identifies letters; `is_palindrome` handles moving the pointers and checking them. The pointer checks let digits count too cos the sentence rule only tells us to ignore spaces/punctuation.

The recursive bonus replaces each pointer movement with another call. Left points at punctuation? Call again with left increased. Right points at punctuation? Call again with right decreased. A mismatch returns False; meeting/crossing returns True. It does the same work without a while loop or sliced strings. I called that variant `is_palindrome_recursive` so both versions stay available to compare.

### 2. gradez again — map makes rows, filter keeps passes

This time map/filter couldn't just be there for decoration. I gave `student_rowx` one student and had it return `(name, average, grade)`. Then `map` genuinely transforms all the student dictionaries into those result tuples. `grade_for` just returns the letter; it doesn't print anything.

`filter` takes those rows and keeps grades A–C. The highest/lowest tracking still uses manual comparisons, and the average still adds scores without sum(). All result containers are made inside the call, so the supplied students stay unchanged. For the bonus I also wrote `[student_rowx(studentx) for studentx in studentsx]`. I find that clearer cos it reads as “make a row for each student”; both versions produce the same list.

### 3. word_freqx again — let the dictionary stay local

The punctuation policy stays the same, but the responsibilities split up. `clean_wordsx` prepares the lowercase words. `count_words` creates a NEW dictionary for this call. `most_frequent` reads the dictionary and returns the winning word without changing it. Output happens after those functions finish.

For the reduce bonus, the accumulator is the winning word so far. Each next word either beats it or leaves it alone. I start with `None` and return an empty string if there are no words. The optional implementation lives in `reduce_bonuz.py` with its permitted functools import, so the main no-import solution stays self-contained. The ordinary loop is still the easier version to explain, tbh 😹.

### 4. two_sumx again — local memory is still pure

Having a seen dictionary doesn't make the function impure. It's created inside `two_sum`, used only for this call, and never edits `nums`. Same input gives the same index pair, and there are no prints or prompts inside. No pair means `[]`, exactly as the question asks.

The reduce bonus carries `(seen dictionary, answer)` through the scan. It fills the first answer and leaves it alone afterwards. Reduce still visits the remaining inputs, but it doesn't keep searching once the answer exists. I update that fresh internal dictionary instead of copying the entire map at each step; repeated whole-map copying would lose the O(n) target. The caller's list is untouched either way.

### 5. second_largestx again — put the update rule in step

This question wants the decision itself as a pure function: `step(largest, second_largest, x)`. So I pass the current two slots and one number into `step`, and it returns the updated pair. The main loop assigns that pair back to its LOCAL trackers.

That's the same largest/runner-up reasoning from Week 3, just pulled into a function we can inspect on its own. The reduce bonus carries the two-slot tuple as its accumulator and performs those decisions in one reduce call. Both ignore duplicate maxima and return `None` when runner-up never gets filled. No sorting or set conversion needed, lol 😼.

### 6. cipherx_again!! — make a function that remembers

`make_shifter(3)` doesn't shift a whole message immediately. It makes and returns a small character function that remembers the 3. That memory is the closure. `map` then calls that returned function for each character, and `join` puts the results together.

Decrypting asks the same factory for a negative shift. Uppercase/lowercase work by choosing the appropriate base code; I never wrote out either alphabet, which covers the bonus too. Punctuation still returns unchanged. The shift comes from the enclosing call rather than a global, so two shifters made with different shifts can coexist without interfering with each other.

### 7. compose_pipex — the order is the whole point

This one had a trap hiding in plain sight: compose and pipe do DIFFERENT ordering. `compose(f, g)` returns a function that does g first, then f. `pipe(f, g, h)` goes left-to-right: f, then g, then h. That's why add-one/double gives 11 with compose and 12 with pipe for input 5.

Each text helper returns a new string. The reverse helper walks indices backwards and gathers characters, cos `[::-1]` and `.reverse()` were banned here too. The recursive bonus handles one last-position character per call until the index drops below zero. An empty pipe returns its input unchanged, which follows naturally from having no functions to apply. The full text pipeline turns “  Hello World  ” into `dlrw llh`.

### 8. flatten_groupx — even the siblings use recursion 😹

“Recursive flatten” sounds simple until they also say no for/while inside it. Recursing into nested lists but looping over siblings would still break that rule. So `walkx` takes an index: deal with that item, then recursively call `walkx` for the next index. If the item is another list, `flatten` handles that list and we extend the fresh result. Otherwise append the item as it is.

`group_by` gets its key rule as an argument, so it can group words by first letter OR numbers by odd/even without changing the function. It builds a fresh dictionary with fresh bucket lists. `combined` connects flattening to first-letter grouping. The bonus makes grouping recursive as well. Empty lists disappear; non-list elements stay. These are finite-list solutions, with Python's normal recursion-depth limit still applying.

### 9. fib_memoz — stop solving the same thing again

Naive recursive Fibonacci keeps recalculating fib(3), fib(4), and loads of other values along different branches. The recursive formula is fine; repeating all that work is what hurts. `memoize` wraps a function with a dictionary cache. The positional arguments form the lookup key, so they need to be hashable.

If the arguments already have an answer, return it. Otherwise call the original function, cache its result, and return that. The cache lives inside the decorator's closure, and each decorated function gets its own. Recursive calls go through the decorated name, so fib(35) only needs the small collection of distinct subproblems. Decorating `count_ways` proves it isn't Fibonacci-specific: one/two-step stair choices reuse the same caching idea. Zero stairs has one way—take no steps.

### 10. transactionz — all the lil functions link up

This one pulls the whole week together. `get_income` and `get_expenses` use the reusable filter helper to keep the relevant rows. `calculate_total` maps each row to its amount, then adds those amounts manually. They said no sum() and no max() lol, so `find_largest` compares each expense with the current winner instead.

`get_balance` subtracts expenses from income. `get_status` handles positive, negative AND exactly zero. `analyze` joins those results into the hard-bonus dictionary, including the largest expense's name and the expense count. Printing stays outside the analysis. Empty transactions give zeros and Break-even; income with no expenses gives largest expense None. Everything works on fresh containers without editing the supplied rows, and the largest-expense helper returns a copy so editing that result doesn't change the source record.

## week 5

### 1. List_Analyxer — start with an actual number

No min(), max() or sum(), so the loop handles all three jobs. `listed` holds the numbers, `minn` and `maxx` start at the first item, and `total` starts at zero. Each `x` gets added to the total and compared with both trackers. After the loop, total divided by `len(listed)` gives the average.

Starting both extremes at zero sounds harmless until every number is negative. Then zero could end up being the largest without even being in the list 😹. Using the first value avoids that. The question guarantees a non-empty list, so `listed[0]` is a valid starting point. The supplied numbers give 5, 27, 66 and 13.2.

### 2. LIneeer_srch — find it, then stop

They banned `.index()`, so `indexxed` starts at -1 and the loop checks `values[i]` against `target`. A match saves `i` and immediately breaks. That break is part of the requirement: this week only wants the first occurrence, even if the same number appears later.

Zero couldn't stand for “not found” cos it's a real list index. -1 works as the sentinel here, and the final check prints a message rather than using it to access the list. For target 7 the search stops at index 1; for 18 it reaches the end with `indexxed` still -1.

### 3. matrixx — reset the right thing

The outer loop picks a `row`; the inner loop adds each `num` into `total`. No sum() involved. `row_num` starts at 1 for the display and increases after each row.

The important bit is resetting `total` INSIDE the outer loop. Leaving it outside would make the second row include the first row's numbers, and that's a running matrix total rather than a row total. With the reset in place, the sample prints 12, 9 and 60. More rows just mean more trips through the outer loop.

### 4. balanced_px — the count needs to stay sensible

Only round parentheses this week, so a counter is enough. Every `(` adds one to `par`, every `)` subtracts one, and everything else in `sentence` gets ignored. A final zero means all the openings got closed, provided the count never went negative.

That last condition matters. `)(` adds up to zero overall, but it's still the wrong order. Breaking as soon as `par` goes negative catches the unmatched closing bracket before a later opening can hide it. An empty sentence has no unmatched parentheses and prints Balanced.

### 5. mini_todo_lixt_lol — task numbers aren't indices

`tasks` is a list of strings and `choice` picks the add/view/remove/quit branch. Add appends the task text; view loops through the list and displays `i + 1`. The while loop keeps the menu going until quit.

Removal is where the two numbering systems meet: task 1 lives at index 0. I check that `number` is numeric AND within 1 through `len(tasks)` before popping `int(number) - 1`. The empty-list checks stop view/remove from pretending there's a task available. After removal the next view numbers the remaining tasks from 1 again.

## week 6

### 1. List_analyxer_againn_wtf — same list, more receipts 😭

The `listed`, `minn`, `maxx`, `total` and `average` work is the same as Week 5. Now `minn_index` and `maxx_index` move with their values. The comparisons stay strictly `<` and `>`, so another 27 doesn't overwrite the index of the first 27. The sample's largest value stays at index 2.

Counting above-average numbers has to wait. The final average isn't known halfway through the list, so counting against a changing average would answer a different question. The first pass gets the total and extremes; the second counts values above the completed average. Here that's 14, 27 and 27: three values, with the average displayed as 13.71. The comparison uses the unrounded average.

### 2. lin_srch — the break had to go

Week 5 stopped at the first match. This version needs ALL occurrences, so `indexxed` becomes a list and each matching `i` gets appended. `values`, `target` and the index-based loop stay familiar; the changed requirement doesn't need a whole new vocabulary.

The list already gives all the output pieces: `len(indexxed)` is the count, item 0 is the first match, and item -1 is the last. Those accesses happen only after checking the list isn't empty. Target 7 gives `[1, 4, 6]`; target 22 gives one index serving as both first and last. No `.index()`, `.count()` or comprehension needed.

### 3. matrix_sum — keep one total per column

`matrixx`, `row`, `row_num` and `total` carry over from Week 5. The row sum still resets each time, and dividing it by the row length gives that row's average. The new part is `column_totals`: one zero-initialised slot for every column.

Inside the nested loop, each value goes into the current row total AND its column's slot. Unlike the row total, the column slots survive between rows. That gives 13, 27 and 41 for the sample. Reading the column count from the first row lets the same loops handle different rectangular dimensions, without hardcoding three columns or importing anything.

### 4. balance() — counting alone can't catch this one

Week 5's `par` counter can't distinguish `([)]` from correctly nested brackets. The totals cancel, but the types close in the wrong order. So this version keeps `sentence` and `x`, then uses a list stack to remember the actual opening brackets.

An opening gets appended. A closing bracket needs a non-empty stack, then its partner in `pairs` must match the popped opening. Last open, first closed 😼. Any mismatch stops the scan; leftover openings also fail the final check. Non-bracket characters never enter the stack, so expressions can contain ordinary text without changing the result.

### 5. todo_lisit — the task needs a status now

A plain task string can't remember whether it's done, so each entry becomes `[text, done]`. Adding starts it at False. Viewing prints `[ ]` or `[x]` from that flag, and the done command changes the flag without removing the text. The familiar `tasks`, `choice`, `number` and `i` still do their original jobs.

Commands now arrive on one line, so splitting once keeps the whole task description together. `get_task_index` centralises the empty-list, non-numeric and range checks for both remove and done. It returns -1 for invalid input; the calling branches check that before touching a task. Otherwise, subtracting one converts the displayed task number into the actual index.

### 6. lip_year — centuries have an exception

Lunar calendar aside, the exercise uses Gregorian leap-year rules lol 🌙. Divisible by 4 isn't enough: 1900 passes that test but isn't a leap year. Divisibility by 400 gets checked first, then 100, then 4, with the remaining years returning False.

That order lets 2000 through before the century rejection catches 1900. The stretch starts at `year + 1`, calls the same checker and keeps collecting until it has three leap years. Starting after the input matters; entering a leap year shouldn't include that year among the NEXT three.

### 7. duplicatez — print each repeated value once

No set(), `.count()` or sorting. The outer loop picks a `number`; the inner loop counts matching `other` values manually. A count above one means it repeats, and `number not in duplicates` stops it being appended again. This is a straightforward O(n²) approach for these practice lists.

The order follows the first position of each repeated value, which matches the sheet's `[1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 5, 1] → [1, 2]` example. That's worth spelling out cos detecting duplicates only when their second occurrence arrives would produce `[2, 1]`. For the stretch, a frequency dictionary counts every value, then a second loop keeps only counts above one. Insertion order keeps those keys aligned with the duplicate list.

### 8. decode_encode — a run isn't the whole frequency

`aabbaa` needs `a2b2a2`, not one combined count for a. So encode remembers `current` and `count`, extending the count while adjacent characters match. A change saves the completed run and starts another. The last run gets saved after the loop too; otherwise it would never reach the result. Empty input returns an empty string, and A/a remain distinct.

Decode remembers a `letter` and collects its count into `digits`. It doesn't expand the run until the next non-digit character arrives, so 12 stays twelve rather than being read as separate counts. The final run is expanded after the loop. The format works for non-digit source characters; literal digits would need an escaping rule to avoid being mistaken for counts. No groupby or imports involved.
